# Logging Standards

Logging is infrastructure. It is configured once, in `app/core/logging.py`
(`configure_logging()`, called from `main.py` lifespan startup), and used
everywhere else through the same façade.

---

# The Logger

Always

```python
import structlog

logger = structlog.get_logger(__name__)
```

Never

```python
import logging

logger = logging.getLogger(__name__)
```

Never

```python
print(...)
```

`structlog.get_logger(__name__)` works identically inside routers, use cases,
repositories, and domain code — it does not import FastAPI, SQLAlchemy, or
anything else framework-specific, so using it does not violate the
framework-independence rules in `07-use-cases.mdc` or `08-repositories.mdc`.

---

# Structured Events, Not Strings

Log an event name plus keyword fields. Never interpolate values into the
message string.

Good

```python
logger.warning("student_already_enrolled", student_id=student.id, exam_id=exam.id)
```

Bad

```python
logger.warning(f"Student {student.id} is already enrolled in exam {exam.id}")
```

The event name is a stable, greppable identifier (snake_case, like an error
code). Values belong in fields, not in the sentence.

---

# request_id vs correlation_id

`RequestContextMiddleware` (`app/core/middleware.py`) binds both to
`structlog.contextvars` for the lifetime of every request. Every log call
made anywhere during that request automatically includes them — never pass
`Request` into a use case or repository just to read an id back out.

- `request_id` — identifies this one hop (this FastAPI process, this
  request). Always freshly generated here. Use it to debug what happened
  inside this service.
- `correlation_id` — the id that must survive across process/service
  boundaries (Next.js → FastAPI → queue → worker → email). Read from an
  incoming `X-Correlation-ID` header when present, otherwise generated. When
  a use case later publishes to a queue, calls an external API, or triggers
  a background job, propagate `correlation_id` (read it via
  `structlog.contextvars.get_contextvars()`) to the outbound call/message so
  every hop's logs stay joinable on that one field, per `04-async.mdc`'s
  guidance to use `ContextVar` for request-scoped tracing context instead of
  globals or manual parameter threading.

---

# Three Event Types

Do not confuse business events, application failures, and HTTP access logs.
They answer different questions and are not duplicates when named distinctly.

| Event type | Example | Who logs |
|---|---|---|
| Business event | `exam_approval_failed`, `payment_declined` | Use case (optional) |
| Application failure | `application_exception` | Global handler (once) |
| HTTP access | `request_completed` | Middleware (once) |

**Default for propagated failures:** attach context via `log_context` on the
exception; the handler logs once. A separate `logger.*` before `raise` is
allowed only when recording a **distinct** business or infrastructure event
whose meaning is not captured by `application_exception` alone.

---

# Exception Logging

Full mechanics — single log line per exception, `log_context` merged from
the full `__cause__` chain (inner layers first, outer wins on conflict),
reserved field names (`code`, `message`, `status_code`, `exc_info`, and the
`cause_*`/`root_cause_*` fields) — live in `12-errors.mdc`'s "Logging" and
"Log Context vs Details" sections; this file only adds the event-type
framing above ("Three Event Types"). A separate `logger.*` before `raise`
is permitted only for a distinct business or infrastructure event, never
as a restatement of the failure itself.

---

# One Line Per Request

The middleware emits exactly one line per request. Normally that is
`request_completed` at **INFO**, with `status_code`, `duration_ms`, and
`outcome` (`success` / `client_error` / `server_error`). This is the
project's HTTP access log — do not duplicate it in routers or use cases.
Use `outcome` and `status_code` for filtering; do not promote access logs
to WARNING or ERROR based on status code.

If `call_next()` itself raises — e.g. `asyncio.CancelledError` on a client
disconnect, which is a `BaseException`, not an `Exception`, so
`unhandled_exception_handler` (registered for `Exception` only) never sees
it — the middleware logs `request_cancelled` at WARNING with `duration_ms`
instead, then re-raises unchanged. Never swallow it: `04-async.mdc` requires
cancellation to propagate. Without this, such a request would produce zero
log lines, silently breaking the "exactly one line per request" guarantee.

`request_started` is logged at **DEBUG only**. It exists for local
troubleshooting. At the default `LOG_LEVEL=INFO` it never renders, which
matters at scale: doubling every request into a `started` + `completed` pair
doubles log volume for no operational benefit once `completed` already
carries the status and timing. Never promote `request_started` to INFO.

---

# Tracebacks on Application Failures

Full exception tracebacks (`exc_info`) on `application_exception` are
included only when `settings.debug` is true. `LOG_LEVEL` is a separate
knob — it controls which events are emitted, never traceback verbosity.
Regardless of `debug`, the handler always logs compact `cause_type`/
`cause_message` (immediate cause) and `root_cause_type`/`root_cause_message`
(innermost cause, when different) — see `12-errors.mdc`'s "Exception
Chaining". `unhandled_exception` always includes `exc_info` (CRITICAL),
since for a truly unanticipated failure the traceback is the only
diagnostic signal available.

---

# Log Levels

Match the exception-to-level table in `12-errors.mdc` exactly:

| Category | Level |
|---|---|
| Validation (`ValidationException`, `RequestValidationError`) | INFO |
| Business failures (`AuthenticationException`, `AuthorizationException`, `NotFoundException`, `ConflictException`, `BusinessRuleViolation`) | WARNING |
| Infrastructure failures (`PersistenceException`, `ExternalServiceException`, `ServiceUnavailableException`) | ERROR |
| Unexpected/unhandled exceptions | CRITICAL |

This mapping lives in `app/core/exception_handlers.py`
(`_LOG_METHOD_BY_EXCEPTION`). Do not reintroduce ad hoc `>=500` / `>=400`
branching — extend the table instead when adding new exception types.

The one sanctioned exception is `http_exception_handler`, which logs
`StarletteHTTPException` by status-code bucket because that type was never
part of `_LOG_METHOD_BY_EXCEPTION` — it carries no business meaning to key a
level off of. See `12-errors.mdc`'s "HTTPException Logging".

---

# Never Log

Passwords, tokens, cookies, `Authorization` headers, API keys, secrets,
credit card data, or other personal identifiers — see `15-security.mdc`.
`configure_logging()` runs every event through a redaction processor that
masks known sensitive keys as a safety net, but that is defense-in-depth,
not permission to pass secrets into a log call. Never rely on the processor
to save a careless `logger.info("login", password=password)`.

---

# Redaction Is a Safety Net, Not a Guarantee

`_redact_sensitive` recurses into `dict`s and `list`s of `dict`s, matching
keys against a known-sensitive list. It cannot see inside arbitrary Python
objects (ORM models, domain entities, custom classes) — it only understands
mappings and sequences. Never log a domain or ORM object directly:

Bad

```python
logger.info("bulk_import", users=[user_model, ...])
```

Good

```python
logger.info("bulk_import", user_ids=[u.id for u in user_models])
```

Log explicit, minimal, primitive fields. The redactor is the last line of
defense against a forgotten key name, not a substitute for choosing what to
log in the first place.

---

# Validation Errors Carry Raw Input

Pydantic's `exc.errors()` includes the raw offending value under `input` for
every field, including ones like `password`. `_redact_sensitive` cannot help
here — the key is `input`, not `password`, so it never matches
`_SENSITIVE_KEYS`. `request_validation_exception_handler` strips `input`
from every error before it reaches either the log call or the client
response (`details.errors`). Never reintroduce raw `exc.errors()` output
into a log call or response body — full standards: `12-errors.mdc`.

---

# Cursor and Claude MUST NEVER

Generate `print()` for debugging.

Generate `logging.getLogger(...)` — use `structlog.get_logger(__name__)`.

Generate f-string/`%s`-interpolated log messages — use event + keyword fields.

Generate `request_started` at INFO or above.

Generate a second per-request access-log line outside `RequestContextMiddleware`.

Generate passwords, tokens, cookies, or secrets as log fields.

Generate manual `request_id`/`correlation_id` parameters threaded through use
cases or repositories — read them from `structlog.contextvars` instead.

Generate log level branching that diverges from the `12-errors.mdc` table
(outside the sanctioned `http_exception_handler` status-bucket exception).

Generate a domain or ORM object as a raw log field value — log explicit
primitive fields instead.

Generate raw `exc.errors()` (with `input` intact) into a log call.

Generate a second `logger.*` call for a failure the global exception handler
will already log — use `log_context` on the exception instead.

---

# Final Principle

Logging is one pipeline, configured once, used the same way at every layer.
`request_id` and `correlation_id` travel automatically via contextvars so
business code never needs to know about HTTP. Log levels are a fixed
function of exception type, not a per-call judgment call.
